# The build context is otherwise ~4 GB, almost all of it things the image must
# not contain: the local virtualenv, the 440 MB source shard, three built
# indexes, and node_modules. Docker uploads the whole context to the daemon
# before the first instruction runs, so this file is the difference between a
# build that starts in a second and one that starts in several minutes.

.git/
.venv/
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/

# Secrets. Never in an image, even one that is never pushed.
.env

# The index is fetched or built by the Dockerfile itself -- see the comment
# there. Copying it from the context would also mean copying data/raw, which is
# the 440 MB parquet plus a 55 MB rows cache.
data/

# Frontend: built by the node stage in deploy/Dockerfile and served by the API
# from the same origin, so the *sources* must reach the build context. What must
# not is everything generated -- node_modules is ~400 MB, .next another ~200 MB,
# and Docker uploads the whole context before the first instruction runs.
#
# public/ is excluded too, and deliberately: its six files (the VAD worklet, the
# Silero ONNX weights, the onnxruntime WASM binaries -- 43 MB) are not in git,
# they are produced by `npm run vad-assets` out of node_modules. Excluding them
# keeps the context small and makes the image's copies provably come from the
# pinned dependency rather than from whatever happens to be on a laptop.
web/node_modules/
web/.next/
web/out/
web/public/

# Reports are evidence for the README, not runtime inputs.
reports/
plan.md

# Docs and tooling that do not affect the running service.
docs/
deploy/
tests/
.github/
*.md
!README.md
